From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: reserve the memshare region

The modem asks the application processor for a shared heap once, during
bring-up, over QMI service 52. Stock answers 5 MiB. This port answers 0,
and it answers 0 because there is nowhere to answer from: no reservation
exists, so every byte the daemon could name is memory Linux is already
using. Add the reservation, so that the honest answer can become the
stock one.

What stock actually declares. From the extracted stock vendor DTB, the
memshare node has three clients and exactly one of them is non-zero:

	qcom,client_3 {
		qcom,peripheral-size = <0x500000>;
		qcom,client-id = <0x01>;
		qcom,allocate-on-request;
		memory-region = <&memshare_region>;
		label = "modem";
	};

	memshare_region {
		compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
		no-map;
		alloc-ranges = <0 0 0 0xffffffff>;
		alignment = <0 0x100000>;
		size = <0 0x800000>;
	};

The other two clients declare qcom,peripheral-size = <0x0>.

Why qcom,allocate-on-request does not mean "answer zero". In the vendor
driver, drivers/soc/qcom/memshare/msm_memshare.c, init_size is assigned
from qcom,peripheral-size unconditionally at probe -- allocate-on-request
only defers the *allocation*, not the advertised size -- and
handle_query_size_req() answers init_size. So a stock kernel with this
exact node answers 5 MiB to MEM_QUERY_SIZE for client-id 1 before a
single byte has been allocated.

What the modem on this device asks for, measured at boot
(scripts/modem/memshare-probe.c, then userspace/modem/memshare-daemon.c):

	memshare: request from node=0 port=8 MEM_QUERY_SIZE (0x0024) txn=1
	memshare: raw 00010024000e000104000100000010040000000000
	                             ^^ client_id = 1   ^^ proc_id = 0

One query, for client-id 1, which is the one client stock gives 5 MiB.
This port replies:

	memshare: reporting size 0

so the single question the modem asks about the single client that has a
size gets the one answer stock never gives. Told there is nothing, the
modem does not follow up; told 5 MiB, it follows up immediately with
MEM_ALLOC_GENERIC carrying num_bytes = 0x500000, client_id = 1,
alloc_contiguous = 1.

Where the region goes, and why it is safe. This tree has no dynamically
placed reserved regions at all -- every one has a fixed address -- so the
free holes are stable across boots and can be measured once. On the
device, /proc/iomem:

	86500000-8aa1bfff : reserved        <- ends with pil-gpu-ucode
	8aa1c000-8b7fffff : System RAM      <- the hole, 13.89 MiB
	8b800000-9b7fffff : reserved        <- pil-mpss-wlan, 256 MiB

pil_gpu_micro_code is 0x8aa1a000 + 0x2000 and ends at 0x8aa1c000;
pil_mpss_wlan begins at 0x8b800000. 0x8ab00000 is 1 MiB aligned as stock
requires, 8 MiB is stock's size, and the region ends at 0x8b300000, five
megabytes clear of the modem's own reservation. Both neighbours were read
back from /proc/device-tree/reserved-memory/*/reg rather than assumed.

The region is deliberately given stock's 8 MiB and not the client's
5 MiB: the size in the vendor tree is the size of the pool, the 5 MiB is
one client's slice of it, and matching the pool keeps the shape of the
map the same as the one the modem firmware was built against.

This is a reservation only. It changes no driver and binds to nothing:
with the shipped userspace the daemon still answers 0 and the eight
megabytes simply sit unused, which makes the change safe to carry
independently of whether anything is ever handed out. What it costs is
8 MiB of the 2 GiB the board has.

Two things this patch is not. It is not a memshare implementation: there
is no in-tree client for the node, the responder is userspace. And it is
not sufficient on its own to hand the buffer over safely -- the vendor
driver calls shared_hyp_mapping() -> hyp_assign_phys() to move the pages
from VMID_HLOS to VMID_MSS_MSA before the modem touches them, and nothing
in this port does that yet. Reserving the memory is the part that has to
happen first and the part that has to be in the device tree.

Verified against the running device before writing: no memshare node
exists under /proc/device-tree/reserved-memory/, and 0x8ab00000-0x8b2fffff
is currently plain "System RAM".

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -185,6 +185,30 @@
 			reg = <0x0 0xaefd2000 0x0 0x2e000>;
 			no-map;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The modem's memshare heap. Stock reserves this as a
+		 * dynamically placed shared-dma-pool; here it is a fixed
+		 * address, because the responder for QMI service 52 is a
+		 * userspace daemon that has nothing to allocate from and only
+		 * needs a number to hand over.
+		 *
+		 * 0x8ab00000 sits in the hole between pil_gpu_micro_code
+		 * (0x8aa1a000 + 0x2000, ends 0x8aa1c000) and pil_mpss_wlan
+		 * (starts 0x8b800000): measured on the device as
+		 * "8aa1c000-8b7fffff : System RAM" in /proc/iomem. It is 1 MiB
+		 * aligned as stock requires, and ends at 0x8b300000, 5 MiB
+		 * clear of the modem region above it.
+		 *
+		 * 8 MiB, stock's size, although the client only ever asks for
+		 * 5 MiB: keeping the region the same size as stock's means the
+		 * hole this port leaves in the map is the hole the firmware
+		 * was built against.
+		 */
+		memshare_mem: memshare@8ab00000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0x8ab00000 0x0 0x800000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
 	};
 
 	panel_vci: panel-vci-regulator {
